A||||2||Songman||Headlight motor rebuild||||14||
Z||000000||Songman||10-08-2000||08:46 PM||||Just did the headlight motor rebuild on my 88 coupe today. It went off without a hitch! I used the kit from Rodney Dickman. It came with very thorough instructions, but it is pretty much self-explanatory once you get in there anyway...<P>I did the driver's side first. It was a breeze. Although I did stop midway and go to the parts store for some Form-a-gasket and grease. There is no way that you can save a 12 year old cork gasket!<P>The passenger side was a little tougher but it still went off without a hitch. The only problem was that they used the same actuator housing and it was more difficult to take apart. <P>After I got it put all back together I sat out there making the headlights go up and down about 15 times! What a blast! So much better than those wire ties that were holding the headlights up.||||reg||14
Z||000001||My7Fieros||10-08-2000||08:50 PM||||Yup, I did the same thing on 3 of my Fieros. I didnt use Rodney's kit though...(no particular reason, Ive bought things from Rodney and they're great!), I used glue sticks (!)...yes, they work great.<BR>However I do have a prob on my d's side headlight on one of the Fieros..not related to the rebuild though, sometimes motor dosnt spin at all...hmmm.<BR>Anyway, yes it was a breeze! I laugh when I go to look at a Fiero for sale and the headlights are held up by coathanger wire...if only they knew!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000002||theogre||10-08-2000||11:36 PM||||Actually you can save the gaskets. I saved both mine. It does take patience and gaskets that have some desire to live.||||reg||1
